      "answer": "$m=0.374\\,\\mathrm{kg}$",
      "official_mark_scheme_text": "KE and PE for $m$ kg particle: $\\frac12m(0.36)=0.18m$ and $mg\\sin45^\\circ=5\\sqrt2m$ — B1 for any 2 correct PE or KE terms.\nKE and PE for $0.5$ kg particle: $\\frac12(0.5)(0.36)=0.09$ and $0.5g\\sin30^\\circ=2.5$ — B1 for all 4 correct PE and KE terms.\nApply the work-energy equation to the system as PE loss plus work done by $0.8$ N equals KE gain plus $0.5$ — M1. Guidance: Must include at least 5 relevant terms only and no extra terms; all dimensionally correct.\n$0.5g(1)\\sin30^\\circ-mg(1)\\sin45^\\circ+0.8(1)=\\frac12(0.5+m)(0.36)+0.5$ — A1. May be seen as $2.5-5\\sqrt2m+0.8=0.09+0.18m+0.5$.\n$m=0.374$ — A1.\nAlternative method 1: $0.18m$ and $5\\sqrt2m$ — B1; $a=0.18$ and $3.3-T=0.5(0.18)$ leading to $T=3.21$ — B1; for the $m$ kg particle, work done by $T=$ KE gain $+$ PE gain $+0.5$ — M1, at least 3 relevant terms including tension, all dimensionally correct; $3.21(1)=\\frac12m(0.36)+mg\\sin45^\\circ+0.5$ — A1; $m=0.374$ — A1.\nAlternative method 2: $0.18m$ and $5\\sqrt2m$ — B1 for any 2 correct PE or KE terms; $0.09$ and $2.5$ — B1 for all 4; apply work-energy to both particles with tension terms in both — M1, at least 5 relevant terms and all dimensionally correct: $0.8(1)+0.5g\\sin30^\\circ=\\frac12(0.5)(0.36)+T(1)$ and $T(1)=\\frac12m(0.36)+mg\\sin45^\\circ+0.5$; eliminating $T$ gives $0.8+0.5g\\sin30^\\circ-\\frac12(0.5)(0.36)=\\frac12m(0.36)+mg\\sin45^\\circ+0.5$ — A1; $m=0.374$ — A1.\nTotal: 5.",
